Gave every provider setting its own registry record. Providers used to live in one `pas.plugins.identity.providers` text record holding a JSON list; each one now owns records named `pas.plugins.identity.providers.<id>.<field>`, created and removed as the provider is. A GenericSetup export therefore lists the real fields with the real types — a driver's `int` setting is an `Int` record, a `bool` is a `Bool`, a secret is a `Password` — and the generic registry editor can reach any single value instead of a blob nobody can diff.

Two consequences worth stating. Records live in a BTree and so read back alphabetically, but provider order is visible as the order of the login buttons, so it is stored explicitly in an `order` record rather than inferred. And a provider id now has to be usable as part of a record name: `POST @identity-providers` refuses anything outside letters, digits, `-` and `_`, because a dot would silently split into a further record level and lose the setting rather than fail. @ericof
